{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NYinhT2ZKhKTsMubrP8wBY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Second run confirms APC 0 regression: 1833ms vs 1779ms before (+54ms). This consistently exceeds the 20ms threshold.\n\nThe plan says to try `pool_threshold = 2 × page_size (32 MiB)` as a more conservative alternative. Let me also check which component regressed.\n\nLooking at the breakdown:\nBefore APC 0:\n- STARK excl trace: 1779ms\n- LogUp GKR: 700ms\n- Round 0: 180ms\n\n\n- MLE Rounds: 116ms\n- Openings: 298ms (WHIR: 218, SR: 78)\n- Trace Commit: 476ms\n\nAfter APC 0:\n- STARK excl trace: 1830ms\n- LogUp GKR: 713ms (+13ms)\n- Round 0: 179ms (-1ms)\n- MLE Rounds: 134ms (+18ms)\n- Openings: 320ms (+22ms, with WHIR at 227 and SR at 91)\n- Trace Commit: 476ms (unchanged)\n\nThe regression is distributed across multiple components—LogUp GKR picked up 13ms, MLE Rounds added 18ms, and Openings increased by 22ms, primarily in the WHIR component.\n\nI'm suspecting this is the same VPMM pool state sensitivity issue. With pool_threshold set to 4× page_size (64 MiB) and page_size at 16 MiB, allocations in the 16-64 MiB range are now going through cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. This routing change is likely disrupting the memory layout that was previously optimized for bandwidth-bound kernels downstream.\n\nLet me adjust to pool_threshold = 2 × page_size (32 MiB) instead, which should keep more allocations within VPMM's management and preserve the memory access patterns these kernels depend on.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":849,"cache_read_input_tokens":87454,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":849},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d17da326-31f8-4485-a87a